# A short story for the Aside engine.
#
# SEVENTH REGISTER. Overtime is horror and performance; The Lamp Room is warm and about tending; The Discrepancy
# is quiet and wry; The Water Line is elegiac and practical; Two of Everything is two people and a house.
#
# This one is technical and warm, and it is the first that insists on nothing supernatural. A signalman works the
# last night in a mechanical signal box before the panel at the regional centre takes the line. He is not sad
# about the levers. What goes when the box closes is not the labour - it is the courtesy: the five beats the book
# does not list, because the book records codes and the men had manners.
#
# Six scenes, three endings, one sitting. No variables: the routing carries the whole story, and a flag nothing
# reads is a fault this engine's auditor has found in four scripts now.

title: Bell Codes
author: Roxanne
start: box

== box ==
bg box_interior
music fan_hum
Eighteen lever, forty-one. The frame has been the same colour since before you were born, and the paint has gone
the way of everything else here, which is to say it has lasted.

You put your bag under the bench and take your coat off, because the box is warm and the night outside is not.

The notice is on the board where notices go. One week. The box closes, the levers are secured, the panel at the
regional centre takes the line, and the bells go quiet. It has been coming for eleven years. You have read it
eleven times and it has said the same thing every time, which is a quality you can admire in a notice.

* Read it again, the way you would read a diagram. -> routine
* Hang your coat up. It is a shift like any other. -> routine

== routine ==
sfx bell
The line runs north to south through this box and you are the only thing standing between the two.
Two bells from Wrenmoor means a train has entered the section. One from you sends it on. Two-two back from
Ashfield means it has arrived. Down the board, lever by lever, and you are the hinge.

The book is on the shelf: BELL CODES. Everything in it is four beats or fewer. You have known it since you were
twenty-three and you could write it out in the dark, and tonight, being the last night, you just might.

At 02:14 the bell rings.

It is not a code. Five beats, the last one held.
Then nothing.

* Take the book down. There is no five-beat code in it, and you want to be sure. -> book
* Answer it. Whatever it was, it wanted an answer. -> answer
* Ring the regional centre. That is what the book is for. -> control

== book ==
bg box_interior_book
sfx book
You take the book down and open it, which you have not done in nine years, and the spine makes a sound like a door
in another building.

One. Two-one. Two-two. Three. Three-three. Four. Six. There is no five. There is not only no five-beat code -
there is nothing above four beats anywhere in it, and the last code page is dated 1938 and signed by a man called
Ackroyd whose handwriting is worse than yours.

* Read the back pages. It is a short book, and it is the last night. -> old_pages
* Put it back. The answer is not in a book. -> answer

== old_pages ==
The back pages are the oldest and they are not codes. They are notes, in three hands, about the codes.

Ackroyd, 1938: "Wrenmoor signs off five. Not in the book. Courtesy."

Below it, 1971: "Wrenmoor still signs off five. Told the inspector it was interference."

Below that, in a hand you know because you have seen it on your own timesheets, your predecessor, 1984:
"Wrenmoor closed today. He signed off five and then the circuit was cut. Nobody answered. I was on the Ashfield
side and I heard it and I did not answer, and I have thought about it every shift since."

The five beats are a man going home. It has never been in the book, because it was never railway business.
It was manners, and the men kept it, and the book does not record manners.

The tapper is quiet under your hand. Whoever it was, they are waiting.

* Send it back. Five beats, the last one held. -> end_courtesy
* Write it in the log and leave it at that. -> end_record

== answer ==
sfx bell
You put your hand on the tapper and send five beats back into the dark, the last one held, the way you would
acknowledge anything. It is not in the book. It is not a fault either.

There is no reply, and you did not expect one, and that is the whole of it: a thing that had been said and had not
been answered for forty-two years has now been answered, and you did it without knowing what it meant, which is how
most courtesies are performed.

* Sit with it. You will never find out, and it was still the right thing to send. -> end_blind
* Go back for the book. You would rather know. -> book

== control ==
bg box_interior
sfx phone
The regional centre answers on the second ring, which is the most impressive thing that has happened tonight.
You give them the time, the section, the beats, and you hear a man write it down.

"Held, the last one? That is not one of ours. Interference. It is a wet night, you get that."

You say yes. It is a wet night. It is in fact the driest night in a month, and you do not say so, because he
wrote it down and that is the record, and you have been doing this long enough to know the difference between
being right and being useful.

* Open the book. You are not finished. -> book
* Leave it. He wrote it down. -> end_record

== end_courtesy ==
bg box_dawn
The dark does not answer, and you find that you did not need it to. Someone kept a courtesy for forty-two years
in the margins of a book that was not for courtesies, and tonight it was answered by a man on the last shift of a
box that is closing, which is the kind of handover the railway has always actually been made of.

The light comes up the way it does, grey from the east and then ordinary. The first train of the morning takes
the section and you send it on with one bell, and Ashfield answers two-two.

You write the five beats in the log before you go, so the record has them, because the record is what there will
be. The book stays on the shelf. It is railway property.

-> END

== end_record ==
bg box_dawn
The log has one line in it that is not like the others. You write the time, and the beats, and then the word
the book uses for a thing you cannot place: UNIDENTIFIED.

At 05:40 the light comes up. The first train takes the section and you send it on with one bell, and Ashfield
answers two-two, and the line is the line again.

Somewhere in the archive there is now a page with five beats on it and no explanation, and in forty years somebody
will find it and wonder, which is the closest thing to being remembered that a signal box has ever managed.

-> END

== end_blind ==
bg box_dawn
You sit with your hand on the tapper and the line doing nothing, which is the job: you are the thing that knows
where the trains are, and for four hours there are no trains, and you know exactly where they are.

At 05:40 the light comes up. The first train takes the section and you send it on with one bell, and Ashfield
answers two-two.

You will never know what it was. It was answered, and it was answered by somebody who did not know what he was
answering, and you have decided that this does not make it less of an answer.

-> END
